Link libpython into the C++ ctest executables and keep it past dead-stripping.

On macOS, conda-forge's Python reports a static LDLIBRARY (libpythonX.Y.a)
via sysconfig, so OpenUSD forces PXR_PY_UNDEFINED_DYNAMIC_LOOKUP ON. In
that mode the USD C++ libraries reference Python C-API symbols (TfPython,
boost::python glue) that are left undefined for resolution at runtime, and
only executables link libpython. The C++ test executables built by
pxr_build_test do not reference any Python symbol directly, and
conda-forge's LDFLAGS pass -Wl,-dead_strip_dylibs, so even when libpython
is put on their link line the linker strips it again. The test binaries
then load without libpython in the process and abort with:

    dyld: symbol not found in flat namespace '__Py_NoneStruct'

(also _PyClassMethod_Type, _PyExc_ValueError). This regressed the whole
C++ ctest suite on macOS going from v26.05 to v26.08.

Link libpython (Python3::Python) explicitly into the test executables and,
on Apple, additionally pass ld64's -needed_library for it, which preserves
the load command despite -dead_strip_dylibs. Verified locally on
osx-arm64: previously failing tests (testTfPyLock, testGfIsFloatingPoint,
testTraceCategory, testSdfGlobPattern) link @rpath/libpythonX.Y.dylib and
run successfully after this change. These test executables are never
installed, so the shipped libraries are unchanged.

diff --git a/cmake/macros/Public.cmake b/cmake/macros/Public.cmake
index 5f6a545..bdb4ca6 100644
--- a/cmake/macros/Public.cmake
+++ b/cmake/macros/Public.cmake
@@ -663,6 +663,24 @@ function(pxr_build_test TEST_NAME)
         ${bt_LIBRARIES}
     )

+    # When PXR_PY_UNDEFINED_DYNAMIC_LOOKUP is enabled (forced ON on
+    # conda-forge macOS, where sysconfig reports a static Python LDLIBRARY),
+    # the USD libraries leave Python C-API symbols undefined for resolution at
+    # runtime and only executables link libpython. These test executables do
+    # not reference any Python symbol directly, so the -dead_strip_dylibs
+    # flag in conda-forge's LDFLAGS strips libpython from them again and they
+    # abort at load with e.g. "symbol not found in flat namespace
+    # '__Py_NoneStruct'". Link libpython explicitly and, on Apple, mark it as
+    # needed (ld64's -needed_library overrides -dead_strip_dylibs) so the
+    # load command survives. These test executables are never installed.
+    if(PXR_PY_UNDEFINED_DYNAMIC_LOOKUP)
+        target_link_libraries(${TEST_NAME} PUBLIC Python3::Python)
+        if(APPLE)
+            target_link_options(${TEST_NAME} PRIVATE
+                "LINKER:-needed_library,$<TARGET_FILE:Python3::Python>")
+        endif()
+    endif()
+
     # Find libraries under the install prefix, which has the core USD
     # libraries.
     _pxr_init_rpath(rpath "tests")
